== cli help
> cli -h
  out|usage: pysubs2 [-h] [-v]
  out|               [-f {srt,ass,ssa,microdvd,json,mpl2,tmp,vtt,sami,whisper_jax,ttml}]
  out|               [-t {srt,ass,ssa,microdvd,json,mpl2,tmp,vtt,sami,whisper_jax,ttml}]
  out|               [--input-enc ENCODING] [--output-enc ENCODING]
  out|               [--enc-error-handling {strict,surrogateescape}] [--fps FPS]
  out|               [-o DIR] [--clean] [--verbose] [--shift TIME |
  out|               --shift-back TIME | --transform-framerate FPS1 FPS2]
  out|               [--srt-keep-unknown-html-tags] [--srt-keep-html-tags]
  out|               [--srt-keep-ssa-tags] [--sub-no-write-fps-declaration]
  out|               [FILE ...]
  out|
  out|The pysubs2 CLI for processing subtitle files.
  out|https://github.com/tkarabela/pysubs2
  out|
  out|positional arguments:
  out|  FILE                  Input subtitle files. Can be in SubStation Alpha
  out|                        (*.ass, *.ssa), SubRip (*.srt), MicroDVD (*.sub) or
  out|                        other supported format. When no files are specified,
  out|                        pysubs2 will work as a pipe, reading from standard
  out|                        input and writing to standard output.
  out|
  out|options:
  out|  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  out|  -v, --version         show program's version number and exit
  out|  -f, --from {srt,ass,ssa,microdvd,json,mpl2,tmp,vtt,sami,whisper_jax,ttml}
  out|                        By default, subtitle format is detected from the file.
  out|                        This option can be used to skip autodetection and
  out|                        force specific format. Generally, it should never be
  out|                        needed.
  out|  -t, --to {srt,ass,ssa,microdvd,json,mpl2,tmp,vtt,sami,whisper_jax,ttml}
  out|                        Convert subtitle files to given format. By default,
  out|                        each file is saved in its original format.
  out|  --input-enc ENCODING  Character encoding for input files. By default, UTF-8
  out|                        is used for both input and output.
  out|  --output-enc ENCODING
  out|                        Character encoding for output files. By default, it is
  out|                        the same as input encoding. If you wish to convert
  out|                        between encodings, make sure --input-enc is set
  out|                        correctly! Otherwise, your output files will probably
  out|                        be corrupted. It's a good idea to back up your files
  out|                        or use the -o option.
  out|  --enc-error-handling {strict,surrogateescape}
  out|                        Character encoding error handling for input and
  out|                        output. Defaults to 'surrogateescape' which passes
  out|                        through unrecognized characters to output unchanged.
  out|                        Use 'strict' if you want the command to fail when
  out|                        encountering a character incompatible with selected
  out|                        input/output encoding.
  out|  --fps FPS             This argument specifies framerate for MicroDVD files.
  out|                        By default, framerate is detected from the file. Use
  out|                        this when framerate specification is missing or to
  out|                        force different framerate.
  out|  -o, --output-dir DIR  Use this to save all files to given directory. By
  out|                        default, every file is saved to its parent directory,
  out|                        ie. unless it's being saved in different subtitle
  out|                        format (and thus with different file extension), it
  out|                        overwrites the original file.
  out|  --clean               Attempt to remove non-essential subtitles (eg.
  out|                        karaoke, SSA drawing tags), strip styling information
  out|                        when saving to non-SSA formats
  out|  --verbose             Print misc logging
  out|  --shift TIME          Delay all subtitles by given time amount. Time is
  out|                        specified like this: '1m30s', '0.5s', ...
  out|  --shift-back TIME     The opposite of --shift (subtitles will appear
  out|                        sooner).
  out|  --transform-framerate FPS1 FPS2
  out|                        Multiply all timestamps by FPS1/FPS2 ratio.
  out|
  out|optional arguments (SRT):
  out|  --srt-keep-unknown-html-tags
  out|                        (input) do not strip unrecognized HTML tags
  out|  --srt-keep-html-tags  (input) do not convert HTML tags to SubStation
  out|                        internally, this implies --srt-keep-unknown-html-tags
  out|  --srt-keep-ssa-tags   (output) do not convert/strip SubStation tags for
  out|                        output
  out|
  out|optional arguments (MicroDVD):
  out|  --sub-no-write-fps-declaration
  out|                        (output) omit writing FPS as first zero-length
  out|                        subtitle
  out|
  out|usage examples:
  out|  python -m pysubs2 --to srt *.ass
  out|  python -m pysubs2 --to srt --clean *.ass
  out|  python -m pysubs2 --to microdvd --fps 23.976 *.ass
  out|  python -m pysubs2 --shift 0.3s *.srt
  out|  python -m pysubs2 --shift 0.3s <my_file.srt >retimed_file.srt
  out|  python -m pysubs2 --shift-back 0.3s --output-dir retimed *.srt
  out|  python -m pysubs2 --transform-framerate 25 23.976 *.srt
  exit 0
